# Combo variant of b9e38dc0 (index 13)
# combo_spec: D2/3+D10/0
# Name: 重力下落+先移后填

import random

import numpy as np

from arc_utils import components_mask, get_bg, neighbors4, neighbors8


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Design analysis for the combo variant
#
# a) Concrete behavior and traced example:
#    - Base task geometry is still one funnel/U wall, one fill-colored seed blob
#      near the closed end, and a background region that later floods through the
#      opening and radiates outward inside the two endpoint rays.
#    - The new object family is explicit singleton movers: every non-bg, non-wall,
#      non-fill 8-component of size 1 is a falling pixel.
#    - Small trace:
#      old base code:
#        1. treat extra colors as static obstacles
#        2. fill the pocket and exterior wedge
#        3. erase shadowed fill behind those static obstacles
#      fused combo:
#        1. detect singleton movers
#        2. drop each mover straight down under gravity, leaving bg behind
#        3. with those new obstacle positions fixed, run the original fill+radiate
#           stage and then the same shadow trimming
#      difference:
#        a mover that starts above the future wedge is no longer blocked by future
#        fill, because it falls first; after landing, that same mover can now sit
#        inside the later radiated corridor and carve a different hole/shadow.
#
# b) Complexity calibration:
#    - Only one new motion rule is introduced: all singleton movers fall downward.
#      The radiate direction still varies over four cardinal cases, so examples
#      visibly separate "gravity axis" from "opening axis".
#    - The generator uses 2-4 movers but a single mover color. That is enough
#      repetition for humans to recognize a class of falling pixels without
#      cluttering the grid or turning the task into color bookkeeping.
#
# c) Hidden-assumption audit against the provided solver:
#    - The original code assumed all extra colors are static blockers during fill.
#      That is false now, so obstacle extraction is split into two stages:
#      singleton extras move first, then the moved grid supplies blockers.
#    - Fill-color inference by "closest non-wall color to the wall" still works
#      only if movers stay farther from the wall than the seed blob. The generator
#      enforces 8-neighbor clearance from non-bg structure for mover start cells.
#    - The original flood starts from the seed positions and assumes the wall is
#      unchanged. That still holds, because only singleton extras move.
#    - The original shadow pass scans the final moved grid, so landed movers can
#      block later radiation exactly where they end up.
#
# d) Placement legality:
#    - Wall construction and seed placement stay within the original numeric bounds.
#    - Movers are placed only on bg cells, one per column, with 8-neighbor spacing,
#      so they remain singleton components and their vertical gravity paths do not
#      interfere with each other.
#    - Candidate movers are rejected unless they drop at least two cells and remain
#      in-bounds the whole time.
#
# e) Visual observability:
#    - Across examples, the wall still identifies the radiate direction, while the
#      extra dots always move downward regardless of the opening direction. That
