# Design analysis for the combo variant (D2/1 + D5/0).
# a) Concrete behavior:
#    The fused scene shows two separate wall sprites of the same color. The seed-bearing wall is
#    the active funnel; a translated copy sits farther along the opening direction and acts as the
#    visible collision target. Small isolated non-wall pixels are movers placed inside the corridor
#    that the fill would normally occupy.
#    - Original code path:
#      1. Choose the wall component nearest the seed.
#      2. Flood-fill its interior and extend the wedge to the grid edge.
#      3. Keep extra non-wall pixels static (or treat them like obstacles in the old generator).
#    - Fused path:
#      1. Still choose the wall component nearest the seed as the active funnel.
#      2. Trace each endpoint ray step by step; if the next ray cell belongs to another wall
#         sprite, stop immediately before that wall instead of running to the border.
#      3. Treat every remaining singleton color as a mover. It travels straight in the same
#         radiate direction and stops at the last free cell before the first wall sprite on that
#         line, clearing its entire traversed trail back to background.
#    Example: with `rad = up`, the original wedge and mover both continue toward row 0. In the
#    fused task, if a second wall sprite sits four rows above the opening, both endpoint rays stop
#    against that sprite and a mover in the corridor also stops four steps earlier, leaving a
#    background tunnel inside the otherwise filled wedge. The same object-to-object gap controls
#    both effects.
# b) Complexity calibration:
#    The change is deliberately narrow: only one extra wall sprite and 2-4 singleton movers are
#    added. There are no color remaps, no multiple stop types, and no reflection/detour behavior.
#    To keep the fusion visible, the generator rejects samples where the blocker does not shorten
#    the wedge, where movers would still end at the border, or where the fused output matches the
#    D5-only / D2-only baselines.
# c) Implicit assumptions audited:
#    - Old assumption: endpoint rays stop only on out-of-bounds. Broken by D5/0; ray tracing must
#      check another wall sprite before stepping.
#    - Old assumption: extra non-wall colors are impassable obstacles. Broken by the mover branch:
#      stage-1 fill must treat mover cells as transparent so the later trail-clearing is visible.
#    - Old assumption: output depends only on one wall component. Broken by the combo; the active
#      component is still the one nearest the seed, but another same-colored component now changes
#      both the wedge extent and mover displacement.
#    - New assumption: mover starts are not the fill seed color and stay farther from the wall than
