+#    - Old assumption: there is one dominant wall color. Broken now; wall
+#      detection must work per monochrome connected component, not by frequency.
+#    - Old assumption: fill color can be inferred globally as "closest non-wall
+#      color". Broken because each sprite has its own seed, and a seed color is
+#      also the other sprite's wall color.
+#    - Old assumption: passable cells are bg or "all pixels of the fill color".
+#      Broken by swapped colors; that would accidentally let one sprite flood
+#      through the other sprite's wall. The new flood only treats the specific
+#      assigned seed component as passable non-bg.
+#    - Old assumption: every extra non-bg pixel is a static obstacle. Broken now;
+#      unassigned singleton components are movers that paint along a matched
+#      sprite's radiate direction after the fill stage.
+#
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    The generator builds two non-overlapping wall components with the original
+#    geometric constraints, keeps each seed blob strictly inside its own funnel,
+#    and places isolated dots only on background cells outside the opening side.
+#    Every mover trail is checked to stay in bounds and to avoid both wall and
+#    seed pixels, so the solver never needs tie-breaking about overwriting walls.
+#
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    The two sprites use different radiate directions, so matching matters in a
+#    visible way. The generator also swaps wall/seed colors between the sprites;
+#    therefore "same source color" and "same border color" predict opposite
+#    trajectories in every valid sample.
+#
+# f) Visual cleanliness:
+#    The generator rejects overlap between wall bboxes, filled wedges, and mover
+#    trails. The output therefore shows two clean fill wedges plus a few straight
+#    same-color streaks, not a dense tangle of layered markings.
+#
+# g) Inferability of color changes:
+#    No logic uses numeric color ids. The only color relation is visible and
+#    structural: an isolated pixel shares color with a border, while the border's
+#    interior seed is explicitly a different color. Because the two sprites swap
+#    those roles, multiple examples support the same deduction.
+#
+# h) New solver assumptions audited against the generator:
+#    main() assumes each wall component has exactly one interior non-wall
+#    component intersecting its pocket, and that every remaining singleton color
+#    matches exactly one wall color. generate_input() enforces both facts by
+#    construction and validates them again through the solver itself.
 
 
